  "provenance": {
    "pattern": "lo-fi hip-hop: a lazy boom-bap — kick one and three-and, snare two and four — played behind the beat with heavy swing, under electric-piano seventh chords and a soft sub",
    "knowledge": "public knowledge of the genre: the pattern as drummers, producers and tutorials commonly write it; no recording was sampled, transcribed from audio or mined",
    "note": "Two bars: kick one, three-and; one, two-a, three-and; the snare on two and four; eighth hats swung to 0.62 and late by a thirtieth of a beat with loose dynamics — the drunk feel the style is known for. The chords are stacked sevenths on a warm electric keyboard, one per bar, held; the sub holds the root. The vinyl crackle is the composer's own texture (vinylBed) and cannot be asked for by a row."
  }
